&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-short-22" =
data-mw-group=3D"lower-alpha" id=3D"mw1Q"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" i=
d=3D"mw1g"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mw1w">[</span>a<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket" id=3D"mw2A">]</span></span></a></sup> years ago from the <a =
rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_col=
lapse" title=3D"Gravitational collapse" id=3D"mw2Q">gravitational collapse<=
/a> of matter within a region of a large <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_cloud" title=3D"Molecular cloud" id=3D=
"mw2g">molecular cloud</a>. Most of this matter gathered in the centre; the=
 rest flattened into an orbiting disk that <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Formation_and_evolution_of_the_Solar_System" t=
itle=3D"Formation and evolution of the Solar System" id=3D"mw2w">became the=
 Solar System</a>. The central mass became so hot and dense that it eventua=
lly initiated nuclear fusion in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_core" title=3D"Solar core" id=3D"mw3A">its core</a>=
. It is now classified as a <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/G-type_main-sequence_star" title=3D"G-type main-sequence star=
" id=3D"mw3Q">G-type main-sequence star</a> (G2V). Every second, the Sun's =
core fuses about 600<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mw3g">&nbsp;</span>bil=
lion <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilograms=
" title=3D"Kilograms" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mw3w">kilograms</a> (kg) =
of hydrogen into helium and converts 4 billion kilograms of <a rel=3D"mw:Wi=
kiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass%E2%80%93energy_equivalen=
ce" title=3D"Mass=E2=80=93energy equivalence" id=3D"mw4A">matter into energ=
y</a>.</p>

<p id=3D"mw4Q">About 4 to 7 billion years from now, when <a rel=3D"mw:WikiL=
ink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen_fusion" title=3D"Hydrog=
en fusion" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mw4g">hydrogen fusion</a> in the Sun=
's core diminishes to the point where the Sun is no longer in <a rel=3D"mw:=
WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrostatic_equilibrium" ti=
tle=3D"Hydrostatic equilibrium" id=3D"mw4w">hydrostatic equilibrium</a>, it=
s core will undergo a marked increase in density and temperature which will=
 cause its outer layers to expand, eventually transforming the Sun into a <=
a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_giant" titl=
e=3D"Red giant" id=3D"mw5A">red giant</a>. After the red giant phase, model=
s suggest the Sun will shed its outer layers and become a dense type of coo=
ling star (a <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W=
hite_dwarf" title=3D"White dwarf" id=3D"mw5Q">white dwarf</a>), and no long=
er produce energy by fusion, but will still glow and give off heat from its=
 previous fusion for perhaps trillions of years. After that, it is theorise=
d to become an extremely dense <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Black_dwarf" title=3D"Black dwarf" id=3D"mw5g">black dwarf=
</a>, giving off negligible energy.</p>

