te-bracket" id=3D"mwAkA">[</span>53<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAkE=
">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwAkI">The Sun's original chemical composition was inherited from =
the <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interstell=
ar_medium" title=3D"Interstellar medium" id=3D"mwAkM">interstellar medium</=
a> out of which it formed. Originally it would have been about 71.1% hydrog=
en, 27.4% helium, and 1.5% heavier elements.<sup about=3D"#mwt313" class=3D=
"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-lodders_55-1" rel=3D"dc:references" typeo=
f=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;a=
ttrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;lodders&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-lodders-55" id=3D"mwAkQ"><span class=3D"mw-=
reflink-text" id=3D"mwAkU"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAkY">[</spa=
n>52<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAkc">]</span></span></a></sup> The=
 hydrogen and most of the helium in the Sun would have been produced by <a =
rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Bang_nucleosy=
nthesis" title=3D"Big Bang nucleosynthesis" id=3D"mwAkg">Big Bang nucleosyn=
thesis</a> in the first 20 minutes of the universe, and the heavier element=
s were <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stellar=
_nucleosynthesis" title=3D"Stellar nucleosynthesis" id=3D"mwAkk">produced b=
y previous generations of stars</a> before the Sun was formed, and spread i=
nto the interstellar medium during the <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stellar_evolution" title=3D"Stellar evolution" id=
=3D"mwAko">final stages of stellar life</a> and by events such as <a rel=3D=
"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supernova" title=3D"Sup=
ernova" id=3D"mwAks">supernovae</a>.<sup about=3D"#mwt316" class=3D"mw-ref =
reference" id=3D"cite_ref-hkt2004_78_57-0" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"=
mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&=
quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;hkt2004_78&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&=
quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-hkt2004_78-57&quot;}}"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-hkt2004_78-57" id=3D"mwAkw"><spa=
n class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwAk0"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D=
"mwAk4">[</span>54<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAk8">]</span></span>=
</a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwAlA">Since the Sun formed, the main fusion process has involved =
fusing hydrogen into helium. Over the past 4.6<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=
=3D"mwAlE">&nbsp;</span>billion years, the amount of helium and its locatio=
n within the Sun has gradually changed. The proportion of helium within the=
 core has increased from about 24% to about 60% due to fusion, and some of =
the helium and heavy elements have settled from the photosphere toward the =
centre of the Sun because of <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Gravity" title=3D"Gravity" id=3D"mwAlI">gravity</a>. The pro=
portions of heavier elements are unchanged. <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heat_transfer" title=3D"Heat transfer" id=3D"=
mwAlM">Heat is transferred</a> outward from the Sun's core by radiation rat=
her than by convection (see <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Sun#Radiative_zone" class=3D"mw-selflink-fragment" id=3D"mwAl=
Q">Radiative zone</a> below), so the fusion products are not lifted outward=
 by heat; they remain in the core,<sup about=3D"#mwt319" class=3D"mw-ref re=
ference" id=3D"cite_ref-hkt2004_9.2.3_58-0" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D=
"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs=
&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;hkt2004_9.2.3&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot=
;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-hkt2004_9.2.3-58&quot;}}"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-hkt2004_9.2.3-58" id=3D"m=
wAlU"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwAlY"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket" id=3D"mwAlc">[</span>55<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAlg">]</sp=
an></span></a></sup> and gradually an inner core of helium has begun to for=
m that cannot be fused because presently the Sun's core is not hot or dense=
 enough to fuse helium. In the current photosphere, the helium fraction is =
reduced, and the <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Metallicity" title=3D"Metallicity" id=3D"mwAlk">metallicity</a> is only =
84% of what it was in the <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Protostellar" title=3D"Protostellar" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=
=3D"mwAlo">protostellar</a> phase (before nuclear fusion in the core starte=
d). In the future, helium will continue to accumulate in the core, and in a=
bout 5<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwAls">&nbsp;</span>billion years th=
is gradual build-up will eventually cause the Sun to exit the <a rel=3D"mw:=
WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_sequence" title=3D"Mai=
n sequence" id=3D"mwAlw">main sequence</a> and become a <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLi=
nk" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_giant" title=3D"Red giant" id=
=3D"mwAl0">red giant</a>.<sup about=3D"#mwt322" class=3D"mw-ref reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-59" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-m=
w=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot=
;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-59&quot;}}"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-59" id=3D"mwAl4"><span class=3D=
"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwAl8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAmA">[<=
/span>56<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAmE">]</span></span></a></sup>=
</p>

<p id=3D"mwAmI">The chemical composition of the photosphere is normally con=
sidered representative of the composition of the primordial Solar System.<s=
up about=3D"#mwt325" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-aller1968_60=
-0" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;nam=
e&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;aller1968=
&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-=
aller1968-60&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_not=
e-aller1968-60" id=3D"mwAmM"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwAmQ"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAmU">[</span>57<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket" id=3D"mwAmY">]</span></span></a></sup> Typically, the solar heavy-elem=
ent abundances described above are measured both by using <a rel=3D"mw:Wiki=
Link" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astronomical_spectroscopy" titl=
e=3D"Astronomical spectroscopy" id=3D"mwAmc">spectroscopy</a> of the Sun's =
photosphere and by measuring abundances in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteorites" title=3D"Meteorites" class=3D"mw-r=
edirect" id=3D"mwAmg">meteorites</a> that have never been heated to melting=
 temperatures. These meteorites are thought to retain the composition of th=
e protostellar Sun and are thus not affected by the settling of heavy eleme=
nts. The two methods generally agree well.<sup about=3D"#mwt328" class=3D"m=
w-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-basu2008_61-0" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=
=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;at=
trs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;basu2008&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;i=
d&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-basu2008-61&quot;}}"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-basu2008-61" id=3D"mwAmk"><span =
class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwAmo"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"m=
wAms">[</span>58<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAmw">]</span></span></=
a></sup></p>

