f=3D"mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;templa=
testyles&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;Module:Hatnote/styl=
es.css&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;extsrc&quot;:&quot;&quot;}}"></span><=
div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about=3D"#mwt=
399" id=3D"mwAxk">Main article: <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Convection_zone" title=3D"Convection zone">Convection zon=
e</a></div>

<p id=3D"mwAxo">The Sun's convection zone extends from 0.7 solar radii (500=
,000<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwAxs">&nbsp;</span>km) to near the su=
rface. In this layer, the solar plasma is not dense or hot enough to transf=
er the heat energy of the interior outward via radiation. Instead, the dens=
ity of the plasma is low enough to allow convective currents to develop and=
 move the Sun's energy outward towards its surface. Material heated at the =
tachocline picks up heat and expands, thereby reducing its density and allo=
wing it to rise. As a result, an orderly motion of the mass develops into t=
hermal cells that carry most of the heat outward to the Sun's photosphere a=
bove. Once the material diffusively and radiatively cools just beneath the =
photospheric surface, its density increases, and it sinks to the base of th=
e convection zone, where it again picks up heat from the top of the radiati=
ve zone and the convective cycle continues. At the photosphere, the tempera=
ture has dropped 350-fold to <span about=3D"#mwt401" typeof=3D"mw:Transclus=
ion" id=3D"mwAxw" data-mw=3D"{&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&qu=
ot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;convert&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;=
./Template:Convert&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;=
:&quot;5,700&quot;},&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;K&quot;},&quot;3&qu=
ot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;F&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}">5,700</span><span =
typeof=3D"mw:Entity" about=3D"#mwt401">&nbsp;</span><span about=3D"#mwt401"=
>K (9,800</span><span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" about=3D"#mwt401">&nbsp;</span><=
span about=3D"#mwt401">=C2=B0F)</span> and the density to only 0.2<span typ=
eof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwAx0">&nbsp;</span>g/m<sup id=3D"mwAx4">3</sup> (a=
bout 1/10,000 the density of air at sea level, and 1 millionth that of the =
inner layer of the convective zone).<sup about=3D"#mwt402" class=3D"mw-ref =
reference" id=3D"cite_ref-NASA1_64-5" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Ex=
tension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;=
:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;NASA1&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Sun#cite_note-NASA1-64" id=3D"mwAx8"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" =
id=3D"mwAyA"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAyE">[</span>61<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAyI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwAyM">The thermal columns of the convection zone form an imprint =
on the surface of the Sun giving it a granular appearance called the <a rel=
=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_granulation" t=
itle=3D"Solar granulation" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mwAyQ">solar granula=
tion</a> at the smallest scale and <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supergranulation" title=3D"Supergranulation" id=3D"mwA=
yU">supergranulation</a> at larger scales. Turbulent convection in this out=
er part of the solar interior sustains "small-scale" dynamo action over the=
 near-surface volume of the Sun.<sup about=3D"#mwt403" class=3D"mw-ref refe=
rence" id=3D"cite_ref-NASA1_64-6" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extens=
ion/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&q=
uot;name&quot;:&quot;NASA1&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Sun#cite_note-NASA1-64" id=3D"mwAyY"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=
=3D"mwAyc"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAyg">[</span>61<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAyk">]</span></span></a></sup> The Sun's thermal =
columns are <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%=
C3%A9nard_cells" title=3D"B=C3=A9nard cells" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mw=
Ayo">B=C3=A9nard cells</a> and take the shape of roughly hexagonal prisms.<=
sup about=3D"#mwt406" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-76" rel=3D"=
dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&qu=
ot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw=
-reference-text-cite_note-76&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Sun#cite_note-76" id=3D"mwAys"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mw=
Ayw"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwAy0">[</span>73<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket" id=3D"mwAy4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

