</section></section><section data-mw-section-id=3D"19" id=3D"mwBMo" aria-la=
belledby=3D"Other_radiation"><div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=
=3D"Other_radiation">Other radiation</h3></div>
<p id=3D"mwBMs">Solar <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Ultraviolet" title=3D"Ultraviolet" id=3D"mwBMw">ultraviolet</a> rad=
iation ionises Earth's dayside upper atmosphere, creating its electrically =
conducting <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ion=
osphere" title=3D"Ionosphere" id=3D"mwBM0">ionosphere</a>.<sup about=3D"#mw=
t596" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-Phillips1995_127-0" rel=3D"=
dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&qu=
ot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Phillips1995&quot;},=
&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-Phillips=
1995-127&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-Ph=
illips1995-127" id=3D"mwBM4"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBM8"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBNA">[</span>123<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket" id=3D"mwBNE">]</span></span></a></sup> This radiation causes <a rel=
=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunburn" title=3D"Su=
nburn" id=3D"mwBNI">sunburn</a>, and has other biological effects such as t=
he production of <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Vitamin_D" title=3D"Vitamin D" id=3D"mwBNM">vitamin D</a> and <a rel=3D"=
mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_tanning" title=3D"Su=
n tanning" id=3D"mwBNQ">sun tanning</a>. It is the main cause of <a rel=3D"=
mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin_cancer" title=3D"Sk=
in cancer" id=3D"mwBNU">skin cancer</a>. Ultraviolet light is strongly atte=
nuated by Earth's <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Ozone_layer" title=3D"Ozone layer" id=3D"mwBNY">ozone layer</a>, so tha=
t the amount of UV varies greatly with <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latitude" title=3D"Latitude" id=3D"mwBNc">latitude=
</a> and has been partially responsible for many biological adaptations, in=
cluding variations in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Human_skin_colour" title=3D"Human skin colour" class=3D"mw-redirect=
" id=3D"mwBNg">human skin colour</a>.<sup about=3D"#mwt599" class=3D"mw-ref=
 reference" id=3D"cite_ref-128" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extensio=
n/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&q=
uot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-128&quot;}=
}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-128" id=3D"mwBNk"=
><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBNo"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" =
id=3D"mwBNs">[</span>124<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBNw">]</span><=
/span></a></sup> Ultraviolet light from the Sun has <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antiseptic" title=3D"Antiseptic" id=
=3D"mwBN0">antiseptic</a> properties and can be used to sanitise tools and =
water.</p>

<p id=3D"mwBN4">High-energy <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Gamma_ray" title=3D"Gamma ray" id=3D"mwBN8">gamma ray photons=
</a> initially released with fusion reactions in the core are almost immedi=
ately absorbed by the solar plasma of the radiative zone, usually after tra=
velling only a few millimetres. Re-emission happens in a random direction a=
nd usually at slightly lower energy. With this sequence of emissions and ab=
sorptions, it takes a long time for radiation to reach the Sun's surface. E=
stimates of the photon travel time range between 10,000 and 170,000<span ty=
peof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwBOA">&nbsp;</span>years.<sup about=3D"#mwt602" c=
lass=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-NASA_129-0" rel=3D"dc:references" =
typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&q=
uot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;NASA&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot=
;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-NASA-129&quot;}}"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-NASA-129" id=3D"mwBOE"><span clas=
s=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBOI"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBOM=
">[</span>125<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBOQ">]</span></span></a><=
/sup> In contrast, it takes only 2.3<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwBOU"=
>&nbsp;</span>seconds for <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Neutrino" title=3D"Neutrino" id=3D"mwBOY">neutrinos</a>, which =
account for about 2% of the total energy production of the Sun, to reach th=
e surface. Because energy transport in the Sun is a process that involves p=
hotons in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ther=
modynamic" title=3D"Thermodynamic" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mwBOc">therm=
odynamic</a> equilibrium with <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Matter" title=3D"Matter" id=3D"mwBOg">matter</a>, the time =
scale of energy transport in the Sun is longer, on the order of 30,000,000<=
span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwBOk">&nbsp;</span>years. This is the time=
 it would take the Sun to return to a stable state if the rate of energy ge=
neration in its core were suddenly changed.<sup about=3D"#mwt605" class=3D"=
mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-130" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Ex=
tension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;=
:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-130&=
quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-130" id=3D"=
mwBOo"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBOs"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket" id=3D"mwBOw">[</span>126<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBO0">]</=
span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwBO4"><a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Electron_neutrino" title=3D"Electron neutrino" id=3D"mwBO8">Electron neut=
rinos</a> are released by fusion reactions in the core, but, unlike photons=
, they rarely interact with matter, so almost all are able to escape the Su=
n immediately. However, measurements of the number of these neutrinos produ=
ced in the Sun are <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Solar_neutrino_problem" title=3D"Solar neutrino problem" id=3D"mwBPA">=
lower than theories predict</a> by a factor of 3. In 2001, the discovery of=
 <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutrino_osci=
llation" title=3D"Neutrino oscillation" id=3D"mwBPE">neutrino oscillation</=
a> resolved the discrepancy: the Sun emits the number of electron neutrinos=
 predicted by the theory, but neutrino detectors were missing <span class=
=3D"frac" about=3D"#mwt606" id=3D"mwBPM"><span class=3D"num">2</span><span =
typeof=3D"mw:Entity">=E2=81=84</span><span class=3D"den">3</span></span> of=
 them because the neutrinos had changed <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavour_(particle_physics)" title=3D"Flavour (par=
ticle physics)" id=3D"mwBPQ">flavour</a> by the time they were detected.<su=
p about=3D"#mwt610" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-Schlattl_131-=
0" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name=
&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Schlattl&q=
uot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sc=
hlattl-131&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-=
Schlattl-131" id=3D"mwBPU"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBPY"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBPc">[</span>127<span class=3D"cite-brack=
et" id=3D"mwBPg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id=3D"20" id=3D"mwBPk" aria-la=
