<p id=3D"mwBSk">Sunspots are visible as dark patches on the Sun's photosphe=
re and correspond to concentrations of magnetic field where convective tran=
sport of heat is inhibited from the solar interior to the surface. As a res=
ult, sunspots are slightly cooler than the surrounding photosphere, so they=
 appear dark. At a typical <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Solar_minimum" title=3D"Solar minimum" id=3D"mwBSo">solar mini=
mum</a>, few sunspots are visible, and occasionally none can be seen at all=
. Those that do appear are at high solar latitudes. As the solar cycle prog=
resses toward its <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Solar_maximum" title=3D"Solar maximum" id=3D"mwBSs">maximum</a>, sunspo=
ts tend to form closer to the solar equator, a phenomenon known as <i id=3D=
"mwBSw"><a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sp%C3%=
B6rer's_law" title=3D"Sp=C3=B6rer's law" id=3D"mwBS0">Sp=C3=B6rer's law</a>=
</i>. The largest sunspots can be tens of thousands of kilometres across.<s=
up about=3D"#mwt630" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-Sunspot2001_=
135-0" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;=
name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Sunspo=
t2001&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_=
note-Sunspot2001-135&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#=
cite_note-Sunspot2001-135" id=3D"mwBS4"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=
=3D"mwBS8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBTA">[</span>131<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBTE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwBTI">An 11-year sunspot cycle is half of a 22-year <a rel=3D"mw:=
WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babcock_Model" title=3D"Bab=
cock Model" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mwBTM">Babcock</a>=E2=80=93Leighton=
 <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_dynamo"=
 title=3D"Solar dynamo" id=3D"mwBTQ">dynamo</a> cycle, which corresponds to=
 an oscillatory exchange of energy between <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toroidal_and_poloidal" title=3D"Toroidal and p=
oloidal" class=3D"mw-redirect" id=3D"mwBTU">toroidal and poloidal</a> solar=
 magnetic fields. At solar-cycle maximum, the external poloidal dipolar mag=
netic field is near its dynamo-cycle minimum strength; but an internal toro=
idal quadrupolar field, generated through differential rotation within the =
tachocline, is near its maximum strength. At this point in the dynamo cycle=
, buoyant upwelling within the convective zone forces emergence of the toro=
idal magnetic field through the photosphere, giving rise to pairs of sunspo=
ts, roughly aligned east=E2=80=93west and having footprints with opposite m=
agnetic polarities. The magnetic polarity of sunspot pairs alternates every=
 solar cycle, a phenomenon described by <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hale's_law" title=3D"Hale's law" id=3D"mwBTY">Hal=
e's law</a>.<sup about=3D"#mwt633" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_re=
f-136" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;=
name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&=
quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-136&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-136" id=3D"mwBTc"><span class=3D"mw-reflin=
k-text" id=3D"mwBTg"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBTk">[</span>132<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBTo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup abou=
t=3D"#mwt636" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-solarcycle_137-0" r=
el=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quo=
t;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;solarcycle&quo=
t;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-sola=
rcycle-137&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-=
solarcycle-137" id=3D"mwBTs"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBTw"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBT0">[</span>133<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket" id=3D"mwBT4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwBT8">During the solar cycle's declining phase, energy shifts fro=
m the internal toroidal magnetic field to the external poloidal field, and =
sunspots diminish in number and size. At solar-cycle minimum, the toroidal =
field is, correspondingly, at minimum strength, sunspots are relatively rar=
e, and the poloidal field is at its maximum strength. With the rise of the =
next 11-year sunspot cycle, differential rotation shifts magnetic energy ba=
ck from the poloidal to the toroidal field, but with a polarity that is opp=
osite to the previous cycle. The process carries on continuously, and in an=
 idealised, simplified scenario, each 11-year sunspot cycle corresponds to =
a change, then, in the overall polarity of the Sun's large-scale magnetic f=
ield.<sup about=3D"#mwt639" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-138" =
rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&qu=
ot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&=
quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-138&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-138" id=3D"mwBUA"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text"=
 id=3D"mwBUE"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBUI">[</span>134<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBUM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=3D"#m=
wt642" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-139" rel=3D"dc:references"=
 typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&=
quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-tex=
t-cite_note-139&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun#cite_=
note-139" id=3D"mwBUQ"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwBUU"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwBUY">[</span>135<span class=3D"cite-bracket" =
id=3D"mwBUc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

