e to Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. For some periods of several deca=
des (when Neptune and Uranus are in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_(astronomy)" title=3D"Opposition (astronom=
y)" id=3D"mwBtM">opposition</a>) the motion is rather regular, forming a <a=
 rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trefoil" title=
=3D"Trefoil" id=3D"mwBtQ">trefoil</a> pattern, whereas between these period=
s it appears more chaotic.<sup about=3D"#mwt803" class=3D"mw-ref reference"=
 id=3D"cite_ref-178" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data=
-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&qu=
