<p id=3D"mwB1I">As the Sun goes around the galaxy it also moves with respec=
t to the average motion of the other stars around it. A simple model predic=
ts that in a frame of reference rotating with the galaxy, the Sun moves in =
an ellipse, circulating around a point that is itself going around the gala=
xy.<sup about=3D"#mwt902" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-200" re=
l=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot=
;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&qu=
ot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-200&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Sun#cite_note-200" id=3D"mwB1M"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" i=
d=3D"mwB1Q"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwB1U">[</span>196<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwB1Y">]</span></span></a></sup> The period of the=
 Sun's circulation around the point is about 166<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" =
id=3D"mwB1c">&nbsp;</span>million years, shorter than the time it takes for=
 the point to go around the galaxy. The length of the ellipse is around 176=
0<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mwB1g">&nbsp;</span><a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink=
" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parsec" title=3D"Parsec" id=3D"mwB1=
k">parsecs</a> and its width around 1170<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"mw=
B1o">&nbsp;</span>parsecs. (Compare this to the distance of the Sun from th=
e centre of the galaxy, around 7 or 8 kiloparsecs.) At the same time, the S=
un moves "north" and "south" of the galactic plane with a different period,=
 around 83 million years, moving about 99<span typeof=3D"mw:Entity" id=3D"m=
wB1s">&nbsp;</span>parsecs away from the plane.<sup about=3D"#mwt905" class=
=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-201" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"m=
w:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&q=
