<p id=3D"mwCYU">In ancient Rome, <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunday" title=3D"Sunday" id=3D"mwCYY">Sunday</a> was the=
 day of the sun god. In paganism, the Sun was a source of life. It was the =
centre of a popular cult among Romans, who would stand at dawn to catch the=
 first rays of sunshine as they prayed. The celebration of the <a rel=3D"mw=
:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winter_solstice" title=3D"=
Winter solstice" id=3D"mwCYc">winter solstice</a> (which influenced Christm=
as) was part of the Roman cult of Sol Invictus. It was adopted as the <a re=
l=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabbath" title=3D"S=
abbath" id=3D"mwCYg">Sabbath</a> day by Christians. The symbol of light was=
 a pagan device adopted by Christians, perhaps the most important that did =
not come from Jewish traditions. Christian churches were built so that the =
